From 0c4fac36fe35c58dde193fce8f5494e0200c3cdc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Thom Hadfield <100659456+festeraeb@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 03:13:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] netstack: move bridge bytes in place, without staging
 buffers

Each connection staged up to tcp_buffer (1 MiB) in a Vec per direction and
dropped sent bytes from its front, which moves the rest of the buffer on
every send that smoltcp accepts (a few KB per ACK). The bridge was also read
on every pump, a failed recvfrom per pass. Bytes now go straight between the
bridge socket and smoltcp's socket buffers (send/recv with a closure), and
the bridge is read or written only after its readiness event, since mio is
edge-triggered.

Measured on two Arch servers over 1 GbE (P2P path, 64 MiB uploads through
the lemond peer relay): the sending netstack thread used 480 ms of CPU per
request instead of 730 ms, and the wire ran at 100-113 MB/s instead of
~88 MB/s. tests/bridge_transfer.rs wires two stacks back to back and checks
bytes both ways, a half-close and a slow reader.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
